# Warning callout

A warning callout makes an important consequence or misconception visible in reference content.

These are civica reference implementations for component authors. They do not replace native Home Assistant behaviour or demonstrate a connection to a live home.

## When to use it

Use it sparingly when missing the information could lead to a significant mistake. Give it a specific heading and make the explanation self-contained.

## When not to use it

Do not make every helpful note yellow, or use a callout instead of inline form validation. The NHS pattern is researched for information pages, not transactional forms. Use an interruption or confirmation pattern where a journey needs one.

## Home Assistant adaptation

In Home Assistant guidance, make clear that an unavailable reading is not a confirmed safe physical state and that importing configuration may alter existing settings. Do not invent alarm advice or device operating instructions. Link to the owning verified guidance from ordinary surrounding content where appropriate.

## Accessibility and behaviour

Use a heading and explicit wording, with a hidden “Important:” prefix when it adds useful meaning. The fixed warning fill and dark text do not change with the brand palette. Do not depend on a warning icon.

